t doesn't seem to have  a plan so we set out 10 points that we  think the government should  introduce in order to live well with  covert in longer term some are things  they should do immediately some are  longer longer-term objectives but the  goal here is to make sure that we never  have to go into lockdown again and that  we can move forward as a country  protecting public health but doing so in  a way that protects lives livelihoods  and liberties so in fact you are already  preparing for as it were to shift from a  pandemic to endemic yes and i think  that's the sensible thing to do and and  you're right say at the outset we can't  be complacent we're following the data i  was reassured actually by what oliver  dowden said about following the data  because i'll be honest my anxiety  reading the sunday papers this morning  was that this stuff about ending plan b  measures was driven more by the politics  of the situation uh you know boris  johnson's personal political problems  needing to throw some red meat to back  benches who might be writing the letters  in and trying to say oh let's move on  from the downing street parties onto  onto plan b measures and grab some  headlines so um i i want the government  to be motivated by public health and as  you remember from when i came on before  christmas one of the reasons why we  signaled labour support for plan b  measures well in advance of the vote was  to give the health secretary the  confidence to know he had the votes so  my message to him this morning  we've got regardless of all the noise  about boris johnson  and the mess he's caused for his own  party and government let's keep public  health at the forefront of our minds and  let's plan on that basis well it's  rather um difficult to avoid the mess uh  that  as you described it that uh miss  johnson's cause if you want to put it  that way and just in passing i apologize  to viewers if  i brought something personal into into  this discussion let's try and uh keep  above that um now your parties obviously  benefited from the revelations uh that  have been coming out day off today  but let's just clear up one thing  because it came up yesterday um your own  leaders not averse to a quick snifter  during the working days  there's no comparison between  kierstarma following the rules in 2021  and boris johnson breaking the rules in  2020 no comparison whatsoever and i  understand why  the conservative party unable to defend  boris johnson wants to now start going  on the attack and smearing other people  to cause distraction but there is there  is genuinely no comparison between the  two  the two  and i i think  what we've got to do  is to  show could you go back to the point  about the politics that it's not enough  for labour for the government to lose  the next election we've got to earn  trust and win the next general election  and by the way trevor i don't think you  should apologize for um talking about  your personal pain because millions of  people up and down the country are  having the same conversation it's come  up on the doorstep time and time again  in recent days and i think that that  image we saw of the queen isolating the  reason why that image is so powerful is  two fronts  one it showed that people were following  the rules and if her majesty the queen  who is actually genuinely the only  person in the country who is legally  above the law thought it was important  to follow the rules and set an example  why did the prime minister think that  somehow he was above the rules  and and and what the queen has done as  she often does is epitomize a national  mood and a national moment and  she took that responsibility seriously  in the way the prime minister didn't so  i think people are furious and her and  actually one of the uh most powerful  interviews i heard this week was a  caller into a radio program  who  was in bits because he'd enforce the  rules at funerals and was now asking if  he'd done the right thing and felt  terribly guilty about it  and that's what the prime minister  either doesn't understand or doesn't  care about was that people were doing  the right thing we were all in it  together boris johnson and number 10  weren't and that's why he's unfit for  office  against that background and you know  i  we don't have to argue about what you  just said um  it's curious that you know labour is  asked is has said rhetorically prime  minister must resign  but why is there  no vote of confidence why are you  leaving this to tory back benches just  to sort out  well i think there are three routes to  the prime minister going um he could do  the right thing and resign but boris  johnson and doing the right thing rarely  appear in the same sentence he can be  removed by conservative mps  through a motion of no confidence  through the 1922 committee and i  understand more letters are coming in  but they're not there yet or he can be  removed by the electorate those are the  three routes to the prime minister going  and i think what conservative mps need  to weigh up very carefully is whether in  the end they are going to allow a toxic  radioactive prime minister to  contaminate their party because one of  the reasons i push back on the idea that  they're all the same is that when things  like this happen in politics sometimes  the voters do say you're all the same i  don't think we're all the same i don't  think most conservative mps were out  breaking the rules but voters will judge  them if they defend the indefensible if  they stand by the prime minister if  they're complicit by their silence and  that's why they should remove him  because otherwise the voters will remove  them at the next general election hang  on i don't really understand this i mean  one of the routes is a vote of no  confidence in parliament which you could  instigate we could not you do we could  bring forward a motion of no confidence  in the government we've been around the  blocks 